## Bounding boxes

Boxes are `[x0, y0, x1, y1]` in **pixels of the page image they belong to**,
origin at the top-left, already mapped through the scanner simulation — a box on
a rotated, bowed page follows the ink rather than the pristine layout. A box is
the axis-aligned hull of the four mapped corners of the original rectangle, and a
box pushed off the page is dropped rather than clamped.

`reading_order` is derived on the flat page before any warp: Persian is
right-to-left, so it runs top-to-bottom, right-to-left, and it is deliberately
not recomputed from tilted boxes.

Element types are nested — a `table_cell` is a child of its `table`, containment
tested at 86% area overlap because subpixel layout and shared cell borders make a
child overhang its parent by a fraction of a pixel.

## How the split was made

Assignment hashes the **source letter id**, never the page and never a shuffle:

```
position = blake2b(f"{salt}:{source_sample_id}", digest_size=8) / 2**64
```

then walks the cumulative shares. Four consequences, all of them the point:

- **A multi-page letter cannot straddle the split.** The same letterhead,
  signature and synthesised table appear on every page of one letter; splitting
  per page would put near-duplicates on both sides and the eval would read
  several points better than the model deserves.
- **Adding letters never moves an existing one.** Assignment reads only the id,
  so a later export that appends samples leaves every earlier assignment intact.
  A shuffle-and-slice split would silently re-deal the whole corpus.
- **It is reproducible anywhere.** `blake2b`, not Python's `hash()`, which is
  salted per process.
- **Changing the plan is detectable.** The plan is fingerprinted and stored, and
  an incremental export under different shares is refused.

| | |
| --- | --- |
| Plan | `{{ plan.describe() }}` |
| Fingerprint | `{{ plan.fingerprint() }}` |
| Group key | `{{ plan.group_by }}` |

`splits.csv` at the repository root lists `sample_id, source_sample_id, split`,
so the disjointness is checkable without reading any Parquet:

```bash
python - <<'PY'
import csv, collections
groups = collections.defaultdict(set)
with open("splits.csv") as handle:
    for row in csv.DictReader(handle):
        groups[row["source_sample_id"]].add(row["split"])
print("letters spanning >1 split:", sum(1 for s in groups.values() if len(s) > 1))
PY
```

## How it was made

1. **Letter text.** Persian letter bodies drafted by a large language model
   (see [Provenance](#provenance-of-the-letter-text)), with genre, tone and
   whether a table or chart belongs on the page recorded per letter.
2. **Layout.** One HTML document per letter, laid out and paginated by injected
   JS, then rendered by headless Chromium — which is also what reports the
   geometry, so the image, the PDF and the boxes cannot drift apart.
3. **Furniture.** A letterhead with a logo, a religious opener, the
   registration-number / date / attachment block, tables, charts, a handwritten
   signature and a مهر stamp, each attached with its own probability and its own
   RNG stream.
4. **Scanner simulation.** Most pages are passed through a simulated scan:
   paper texture, uneven illumination, a single composed geometric warp, sensor
   noise, soft dust specks, then the greyscale/autocontrast/JPEG pass a scanner's
   own software applies. The bounding boxes go through the same map the pixels
   did.
5. **Export.** This tree.

Every sample records its RNG seed, so any page can be regenerated exactly.

## Limitations

- **Synthetic, so the failure modes are synthetic too.** No real ink bleed, no
  staples, no coffee, no handwriting other than the composited signatures, no
  photocopy-of-a-photocopy generation loss.
- **The letters are not real Persian correspondence.** Phrasing and structure
  follow administrative conventions, but a model trained on this alone will not
  have seen the idiosyncrasies of any real organisation's house style.
- **The logo set is not Persian.** The marks are drawn from an open icon
  collection, so a letterhead may carry a mark no Iranian organisation uses.
- **Bounding-box precision is secondary to page-level text.** Boxes are measured
  from the DOM and are tight, but the corpus was built to improve
  transcription first; a few element types (nested table structure especially)
  are approximations.
- **Tables and charts are synthesised, not parsed from the letter text.** Their
  contents are deterministic from the sample seed and topically consistent with
  the subject, not extracted from prose.

## Provenance of the letter text

The Persian letter bodies were drafted by
{% if text_models %}
{% for model in text_models %}
- `{{ model }}`
{% endfor %}
{% else %}
- a large language model whose id this export did not record
{% endif %}
and the drafting parameters (base URL, temperature, top-p, seed, attempt count)
travel with each row in the `generation` column, so any body can be traced to the
call that produced it. Everything *except* the body — the field values, the
layout, the boxes, the transcription — is produced deterministically by this
repository's own code, never by a model, so the ground truth is not a model's
opinion of what it wrote.

Whatever obligations the model's own licence and acceptable-use terms place on
generated output travel with this dataset; check the model card before using the
text for anything beyond OCR training.

